    "review": "The July 25, 1982 night at Compton Terrace is a high‑octane snapshot of the Dead in full summer stride. From the opening Mississippi Half‑Step into a blazing Franklin’s Tower (despite a few vocal slips) the band rides a relentless groove that never loses its spark. Highlights explode in Set 2 – the classic Scarlet Begonias > Fire on the Mountain, an inspired Crazy Fingers, and a soaring Estimated Prophet, followed by a seamless Black Peter → Good Lovin' transition that feels both playful and profound. Lost Sailor gets an unforgettable lyrical shout‑out from Bob, while Brent Mydland’s keys add lush color to the jams. The dual drummers keep the pulse tight, and overall the setlist flows like a single, breathing improvisational arc.\n\nFor listeners, the audience recording gd1982-07-25.senn421.vita.minches.80279.sbeok.flac16 captures the show with astonishing clarity – you can hear every nuance of Brent’s synth swells and the crowd’s roar as if you were right there in Tempe. Coupled with the solid soundboard source that preserves the band’s dynamics, this night stands out as a must‑hear for anyone chasing that late‑’80s fire.",
